WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.750
A pupil asked me today: “Sir, is there a

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.790
tiny brain inside the computer?”

3
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.700
I said no.

4
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.120
But then I had to explain

5
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.140
what a neural network is.

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.340
Today: one neuron, the building

7
00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.220
block of every neural network.

8
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.000
Every morning Joseph

9
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.520
decides: umbrella or not?

10
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.970
A neuron takes numbers in.

11
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:27.540
Is it cloudy?

12
00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:29.650
Does the forecast say rain?

13
00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:31.350
Is it the rainy season?

14
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.360
Yes is one, no is zero.

15
00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.530
Then it multiplies each input by a

16
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.890
weight: how much it matters.

17
00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.790
Say the forecast weighs three, the

18
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.430
season two, the clouds one.

19
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.830
Today it’s cloudy, rain is forecast,

20
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.210
but it isn’t the rainy season.

21
00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:54.390
One times one, plus one times

22
00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.810
three, plus zero times two: four.

23
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.810
Add the bias, minus one: three.

24
00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.650
Above zero means yes: take the umbrella.

25
00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.470
But who chose those weights?

26
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.560
At first, nobody: they start

27
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.700
random, and the answers are poor.

28
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.080
Training compares each answer with the

29
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.460
right one and adjusts the weights a

30
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:18.620
little.

31
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.360
For a neuron, learning means

32
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.340
changing its weights and bias.

33
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.200
So an artificial neuron multiplies each

34
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.740
input by its weight, adds them up with a

35
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.520
bias, and turns the total into an answer.

36
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.510
It’s not a brain cell: it’s arithmetic.

37
00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.830
And this bias isn’t unfairness: it’s

38
00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:42.970
how keen the neuron is to say yes.

39
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.220
It’s like your school’s marking scheme.

40
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.060
The exam might count three times as

41
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.820
much as homework: a weight.

42
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.420
Add the parts, compare with the

43
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.660
pass mark: pass or fail.

44
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.120
Change the weights, and the same

45
00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.680
pupils get different results.

46
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:01.530
Quick check!

47
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.860
The forecast’s weight is

48
00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.340
three; the clouds’ is one.

49
00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:08.190
What does that tell you?

50
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.970
The forecast counts for more.

51
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.930
A bigger weight pushes

52
00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.130
harder on the answer.

53
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.670
A weight of zero means the

54
00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:17.730
input is ignored.

55
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.040
And a negative weight pushes towards no.

56
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:23.280
There’s a limit, though.

57
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.180
On a chart of examples, one neuron

58
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.880
draws one straight line: yes on one

59
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.500
side, no on the other.

60
00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.490
Now ask: is the classroom comfortable?

61
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.520
Yes in the middle, no when

62
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.860
too cold or too hot.

63
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:39.910
One straight line can’t do

64
00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.450
that: you need two.

65
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.970
In the lab, you set the umbrella

66
00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.150
neuron’s weights and bias yourself.

67
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.770
Your challenge: get all four days right.

68
00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:52.830
Your turn.

69
00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:55.250
Which of these can one

70
00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.650
neuron decide on its own?

71
00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.220
The pass mark: one

72
00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.540
straight line is enough.

73
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.380
Remember: a neuron multiplies inputs by

74
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.080
weights, adds a bias, and decides.

75
00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:09.590
Learning changes the weights.

76
00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.550
And one neuron draws one line, so

77
00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:14.990
harder shapes need many.

78
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.680
Try it in class tomorrow:

79
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.380
be a human neuron.

80
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.080
Three pupils hold input cards, a one

81
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.440
or a zero, each with a weight.

82
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.670
A fourth adds up the weighted votes with

83
00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.490
the bias and decides: umbrella or not?

84
00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.500
Then change a weight, and decide again.
